    "knowledge_point": "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents",
    "problem": "If ${81}^{3m}={27}^{m+5}$ then $m=$",
    "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $81={3}^{4}$ and $27={3}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{4})}^{3m}={({3}^{3})}^{m+5}\\,\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{12m} & ={3}^{3(m+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12m & =3(m+5) \\\\ 12m & =3m+15 \\\\ 9m & =15 \\\\ m & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
